  "contentHtml": "<div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"overview\">Overview</h2><p>The <strong>Airbus A321neo</strong> is the <strong>newest narrowbody</strong> in United's fleet — <strong>62 aircraft</strong> with deliveries continuing through 2026, arriving in United's 100th-anniversary year. It's the capacity replacement for the older <a href=\"/fleet/a319\">A319</a> and <a href=\"/fleet/a320\">A320</a>, adding seats and range while burning 15–20% less fuel thanks to Pratt & Whitney PW1100G (GTF) engines.</p><p>Every A321neo rolls off the line with United's <strong>Signature Interior</strong>: 4K Panasonic seatback screens at every seat, Bluetooth audio, power at every seat, LED mood lighting and larger overhead bins — the most consistent cabin in the narrowbody fleet.</p><div class=\"highlight-box\"><strong>Key facts:</strong> 62 aircraft · 200 seats · Newest type · Factory-fit Signature Interior · 4K seatback screens at every seat · Starlink rollout underway · PW1100G engines · Deliveries through 2026</div></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"seat-config\">Seat Configuration</h2><table class=\"config-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cabin</th><th>Seats</th><th>Layout</th><th>Pitch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>First</strong></td><td>20</td><td>2-2</td><td>37–38\"</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-ep\">Economy Plus</span></td><td>57</td><td>3-3</td><td>34\"</td></tr><tr><td>Economy</td><td>123</td><td>3-3</td><td>30\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"wifi\">WiFi & In-Flight Entertainment</h2><p>Every A321neo pairs its <strong>Signature Interior</strong> — 4K seatback screens, Bluetooth audio and 110V power at every seat — with <strong>Viasat Ka-band WiFi</strong> today. United's free <strong>Starlink</strong> WiFi (free for MileagePlus members) is rolling out across the fleet, part of an upgrade that had equipped more than 425 aircraft by mid-2026 and targets about 1,000 by year-end.</p></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"a321xlr\">The A321XLR — United's New Long-Range Single-Aisle</h2><p>The <strong>Airbus A321XLR</strong> is the long-range member of the A321neo family, and United's first entered service in <strong>June 2026</strong>. It's the airline's first single-aisle jet to offer lie-flat <strong>United Polaris</strong> business suites (20) alongside <strong>Premium Plus</strong> (12), in a four-cabin, 150-seat layout — powered by the same Pratt & Whitney PW1100G family as the standard A321neo. These are United's new standard lie-flat Polaris suites, not the larger Polaris Studio that is exclusive to the <a href=\"/fleet/787-9-dreamliner\">Boeing 787-9</a>.</p><p>United is using the A321XLR to replace the retiring <a href=\"/fleet/757-200\">Boeing 757-200</a> on long, thin routes and to open markets a single-aisle jet previously couldn't serve — early examples include <a href=\"/hubs/ewr\">Newark (EWR)</a>–Edinburgh and Newark–Bogotá. About a dozen XLRs are expected in the fleet by the end of 2026, scaling through 2028.</p><div class=\"highlight-box\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> the A321XLR gives United transatlantic and Latin American reach from a fuel-efficient narrowbody, extending the A321neo family into the long, thin routes once flown by the Boeing 757.</div></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"routes\">Routes & Hubs</h2><p>The standard A321neo flies domestic and medium-haul routes from every United hub — including <a href=\"/hubs/ord\">Chicago (ORD)</a>, <a href=\"/hubs/den\">Denver (DEN)</a>, <a href=\"/hubs/iah\">Houston (IAH)</a> and <a href=\"/hubs/ewr\">Newark (EWR)</a> — with the range for coast-to-coast and longer transcontinental flying. The long-range A321XLR extends the family to transatlantic and Latin American routes.</p><h3>Related Aircraft</h3><p><a href=\"/fleet/a319\">A319</a> (76) and <a href=\"/fleet/a320\">A320</a> (68) are the older A320-family jets the A321neo is replacing.</p></div>"
